---
import data from '../data/carrier-survival.json';

const { carriers, rows, features, baseRate, auc, baselineAuc, lift, ece, gains, segments, repo } =
  data;

const fmtInt = (n: number) => Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-US');
const fmtM = (n: number) => `${(n / 1_000_000).toFixed(1)}M`;
const pct = (n: number, digits = 0) =>
  `${(n * 100).toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: digits, maximumFractionDigits: digits })}%`;

/**
 * The gains chart is the one worth leading with.
 *
 * AUC is the number a modeller reaches for and the number a broker cannot act
 * on. "Review the riskiest 10% of your book and you catch 58% of the failures"
 * is the same model stated as a decision, and it needs no explanation of what
 * an ROC curve is.
 *
 * Both charts are scaled against a true 0-100% axis rather than against their
 * own maximum, because the interesting comparison is with the diagonal — review
 * 10%, catch 10% — not with each other.
 */
const headline = gains.find((g) => g.reviewed === 10) ?? gains[0];

const stats = [
  { value: auc.toFixed(3), label: 'AUC', note: `against ${baselineAuc.toFixed(3)} for a two-column baseline` },
  { value: `${lift.toFixed(1)}x`, label: 'Lift', note: `over a ${pct(baseRate, 1)} base rate` },
  { value: fmtM(carriers), label: 'Carriers', note: `${fmtM(rows)} carrier-months, ${features} features` },
  { value: ece.toFixed(4), label: 'Calibration error', note: 'predicted risk tracks reality to ~0.08pp' },
];
---

<div class="carrier">
    <p class="lede">
      Will a trucking carrier still be operating a year from now? Brokers, insurers and lessors all
      carry exposure to carriers that may not exist at renewal, and every public tool is
      backward-looking. This model scores {fmtM(carriers)} carriers from public FMCSA records. The
      figures below are the real evaluation output, not illustrations.
    </p>

    <ul class="stats">
      {
        stats.map((s) => (
          <li class="stat">
            <span class="stat__value">{s.value}</span>
            <span class="stat__label">{s.label}</span>
            <span class="stat__note">{s.note}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <div class="grid">
      <!-- ---------- cumulative gains ---------- -->
      <figure class="panel">
        <figcaption class="panel__head">
          <h3 class="panel__title">What a review queue actually buys</h3>
          <p class="panel__note">
            Share of all failures caught when carriers are reviewed highest-risk first.
          </p>
        </figcaption>

        <ol class="bars">
          {
            gains.map((g) => (
              <li class="bar-row">
                <span class="bar-row__head">
                  <span class="bar-row__title">Review riskiest {g.reviewed}%</span>
                  <span class="bar-row__value">{pct(g.captured)} caught</span>
                </span>
                <span class="bar-row__track">
                  <span class="bar-row__bar" style={`--pct:${g.captured * 100}%`} />
                  <span class="bar-row__mark" style={`--at:${g.reviewed}%`} />
                </span>
                <span class="bar-row__meta">
                  {(g.captured / (g.reviewed / 100)).toFixed(1)}x better than reviewing at random
                </span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

        <p class="panel__foot">
          The thin rule on each bar marks where random selection would land. Reviewing one carrier
          in ten surfaces {pct(headline.captured)} of everything that fails in the following year.
        </p>
      </figure>

      <!-- ---------- discrimination by fleet size ---------- -->
      <figure class="panel">
        <figcaption class="panel__head">
          <h3 class="panel__title">Holds across fleet sizes</h3>
          <p class="panel__note">AUC by power units. 0.5 is a coin flip.</p>
        </figcaption>

        <ol class="bars">
          {
            segments.map((s) => (
              <li class="bar-row">
                <span class="bar-row__head">
                  <span class="bar-row__title">{s.band} units</span>
                  <span class="bar-row__value">{s.auc.toFixed(3)}</span>
                </span>
                <span class="bar-row__track">
                  <span class="bar-row__bar bar-row__bar--alt" style={`--pct:${s.auc * 100}%`} />
                  <span class="bar-row__mark" style="--at:50%" />
                </span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

        <p class="panel__foot">
          Small carriers are most of the population, so they are segmented at evaluation rather than
          filtered out of training — dropping them would move the base rate, not improve the model.
        </p>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- ---------- the judgment call ---------- -->
    <figure class="panel panel--wide">
      <figcaption class="panel__head">
        <h3 class="panel__title">The hard part was the label, not the model</h3>
      </figcaption>

      <div class="prose">

        <p>
        The obvious target — “did FMCSA revoke this carrier’s authority?” — is wrong, and the data
        says so plainly. Of the revocation proceedings on record, <strong>2,208,586 were
        discontinued against 1,600,771 executed</strong>. Most begin with a lapsed insurance filing;
        the carrier files a new policy and keeps trading. Training on “a revocation happened” means
        training mostly on carriers having a bad month.
      </p>
        <p>
        Exit is therefore defined as a revocation with no reinstatement in the following twelve
        months, with voluntary surrender separated out as a competing risk. Features freeze at month
        zero and the outcome is measured over months 6–18 — a deliberate blackout, because insurance
        lapse triggers revocation about a month later and a near-term model would just rediscover
        FMCSA’s own paperwork instead of finding signal ahead of it.
      </p>
        <p>
        Thirteen silent data defects turned up along the way — a grand total read as a fleet size, a
        monthly export that was three files rather than one, a date format that quietly nulled an
        entire vintage. Each now has an automated guardrail and a regression test. That catalogue is
        written up in full in the repository, including the two defects that were in the
        leak-detection code itself.
        </p>
      </div>

      <a class="repo-link" href={repo}>Read the full write-up and code ↗</a>
    </figure>
</div>

<style>
  /* Sits inside an expanded project card, so it paints no ground of its own —
     the card already provides surface, border and padding. */
  .carrier {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* Was the section intro. The card supplies the heading above it. */
  .lede {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---------- stat tiles ---------- */
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0;
  }

  .stat {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .stat__value {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .stat__label {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
  }

  .stat__note {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---------- layout ---------- */
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(19rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .panel {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-lg);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .panel--wide {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .panel__head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .panel__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .panel__note {
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .panel__foot {
    margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---------- bars ---------- */
  .bars {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }

  .bar-row__head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .bar-row__title {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .bar-row__value {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .bar-row__track {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    height: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
    background: var(--gridline);
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .bar-row__bar {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: var(--pct);
    min-width: 3px;
    background: var(--series-1);
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
    animation: bar-grow 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
    transform-origin: left center;
  }

  .bar-row__bar--alt {
    background: var(--series-2);
  }

  /* Reference rule: where random selection lands (gains) or a coin flip (AUC). */
  .bar-row__mark {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    left: var(--at);
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--ink);
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  .bar-row__meta {
    display: block;
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  @keyframes bar-grow {
    from {
      transform: scaleX(0);
    }
  }

  /* ---------- prose ---------- */
  /* Two columns rather than one narrow measure.
     A single column capped at 68ch inside a full-width panel leaves half the
     panel empty and makes it twice as tall as it needs to be. Columns fill the
     width while keeping each line at a readable length — the constraint that
     max-width was there to enforce in the first place. */
  .prose {
    columns: 2;
    column-gap: var(--space-xl);
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    /* A paragraph split across the column break is much harder to follow than
       an uneven column. */
    break-inside: avoid;
  }

  .prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .prose {
      columns: 1;
    }
  }

  .repo-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .repo-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-hover);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .bar-row__bar {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>
